task: dataflow-complete-panes
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-complete-panes
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfc
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 3's Results (the table that filed this)

why: MeasureWroclawFlow says the engine is 5.5x the hand-written lane
  (121 ms against 22), and the decomposition says where ALL of it is:
  84% of the run is the sliding stop-window sink, not because the
  windowing is slow but because the job makes 362 983 stop panes,
  every partition holds most of them, and the coordinator merges
  ~2.9 million accumulators on ONE thread.

  `OkayLane.parallel` does not parallelise that merge. It AVOIDS it —
  it emits at the slice every pane no other slice can touch and hands
  back only the handful that span a boundary.

what: the same rule, as the engine's. A pane is complete in partition
  i when `p.start > hi(i-1)` (no earlier partition can have touched a
  window starting after every earlier event's time) and
  `p.end <= hi(i) - back` (no later one can touch a window ending at
  or before the least a later event's time can be). Complete panes
  are presented and folded into the terminal AT THE PARTITION; only
  the rest reach the coordinator.

  The engine already computes `hi`: it is the prefix-maximum array
  gathered for the watermark seeding, and the seed IS the lower
  bound. What is missing is `back`, and it is two more columns in the
  same pre-pass — each partition's local backwardness and its
  minimum event time, combined with the exclusive prefix max. That
  combination OVER-estimates `back` slightly, which is the safe
  direction: it declares fewer panes complete, never more.

  `Run` gains `merged`, the number of accumulators that reached the
  coordinator, so the improvement is asserted by the suite rather
  than only visible in a benchmark.

scope: the fan road (`Sink`). `Flows.run`'s single-stage windowed node
  keeps merging everything — it already has an answer for a big merge,
  which is stage 2's exchange, and the fan does not.

predict: the stop sink is 93 of the engine's 111 ms at 8 partitions.
  If the merge is the whole of it, the fan should land near the
  hand-written 22 ms; if it lands at 50-60 the remaining half is the
  per-pane tuple key (`windows-packed-key`) and the answer is a
  second lane, not a bigger version of this one. MEASURE which.
